Summary
1. ConfigMaps
Theory:
•	ConfigMaps store non-sensitive configuration data (like configuration files, environment variables, or SQL files) separately from the container image.
•	Useful for decoupling configuration from application code.
•	Can be mounted as volumes or injected as environment variables in pods.
Commands:
•	Create from file:
oc create configmap dbfiles --from-file=insertdata.sql
•	Verify:
oc get configmaps
•	Mount as a volume in deployment:
oc set volume deployment/file-sharing --add --name=config-map-pvc --mount-path=/home/database-files --configmap-name=dbfiles
________________________________________
2. Secrets
Theory:
•	Secrets store sensitive data like passwords, API keys, or database credentials.
•	Values are base64-encoded, not encrypted — keep in mind for security.
•	Can also be mounted as volumes or injected as environment variables.
Commands:
•	Create a secret:
oc create secret generic world-cred --from-literal=user=redhat --from-literal=password=redhat123 --from-literal=database=world_x
•	Inject into deployment as environment variables:
oc set env deployment/dbserver --from secret/world-cred --prefix=MYSQL_
•	Verify:
oc get secrets world-cred
________________________________________
3. Persistent Volume Claims (PVCs)
Theory:
•	PVCs are requests for storage by a pod.
•	They define capacity, access mode, and optionally the storage class.
•	Pod can then mount the PVC as a volume.
•	Access modes:
o	ReadWriteOnce (RWO) – One pod can read/write.
o	ReadOnlyMany (ROX) – Multiple pods can read.
o	ReadWriteMany (RWX) – Multiple pods can read/write.
Commands:
•	Add PVC to deployment:
oc set volume deployment/web-server --add --name=web-pv --type=persistentVolumeClaim --claim-mode=rwo --claim-size=5Gi --mount-path=/usr/share/nginx/html --claim-name=web-pv-claim
•	Verify PVC:
oc get pvc
________________________________________
4. Volume Modes
Theory:
•	Kubernetes supports two volume modes for PVs:
1.	Filesystem – Standard file system, most common.
2.	Block – Raw block device access, can improve performance (used for databases).
Commands:
•	Specify block volume via storage class (example lab):
volumeClaimTemplates:
  - metadata:
      name: data
    spec:
      accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
      storageClassName: "lvms-vg1"
      resources:
        requests:
          storage: 1Gi
________________________________________
5. Storage Classes
Theory:
•	Define types of storage in a cluster (e.g., NFS, local disk, SSD).
•	Enable dynamic provisioning: PVC automatically creates a PV using the storage class.
•	Storage classes can optimize for performance, availability, or replication.
Commands:
•	Use default storage class when creating PVC (no --claim-class specified):
oc set volume deployment/web-server --add --name=web-pv --claim-size=5Gi ...
•	Specify storage class explicitly:
--claim-class nfs-storage
________________________________________
6. Reclaim Policy
Theory:
•	Determines what happens to PV data after PVC is deleted. Common policies:
o	Delete – PV and data are deleted.
o	Retain – PV remains, data is safe.
o	Recycle – PV is scrubbed and made available for reuse.
Commands:
•	Check PVC and PV:
oc get pvc
oc get pv
________________________________________
7. Stateful Sets
Theory:
•	Represent stateful applications with stable, unique identities (hostnames, network IDs).
•	Each pod gets its own unique PV, unlike deployments where replicas share PVC.
•	Useful for databases, caches, or any app that requires persistent state.
Commands:
•	Create a stateful set:
oc create -f statefulset-db.yml
•	Verify pods:
oc get pods -l app=database -o wide
•	Check PVs per pod:
oc get pvc -l app=database
________________________________________
8. Practical Differences: Deployment vs Stateful Set
Feature	Deployment	StatefulSet
Pod identity	Dynamic	Stable, unique
PVC per pod	Shared	One PVC per pod
Use case	Stateless apps	Databases, caches, etc.
________________________________________
✅ Quick Tip:
•	Deployments + shared PVC → good for web servers serving static content.
•	StatefulSets + unique PVC per pod → required for databases to preserve individual pod data.

